---
title: Build for yourself first
date: 2026-07-06
summary: I build tools to make my own life easier before I build them for anyone else. If something genuinely solves my problem, it usually solves someone else's too.
draft: false
---

A lot of my side projects start selfishly. I hit some small, annoying problem in my own day, and instead of living with it, I build the thing that fixes it.

That is on purpose.

## The best brief is your own problem

When you build for yourself, you are the user. You know exactly what is annoying, what "good enough" looks like, and when you are cutting a corner that matters. There is no guessing about the requirements, because you feel them every day. You ship it, you use it, and the feedback is immediate.

A lot of tools fail because they solve a problem nobody actually has. Building for myself removes that risk before I write a line.

## If it works for me, it works for someone else

Here is the part that makes it more than a hobby. I am not that unusual. If something genuinely saves me time, there is almost always someone else out there with the same annoyance who would use the same fix.

So a personal tool is a small, honest test. If I keep using it after the novelty wears off, that is a real signal. If I quietly stop, that is a signal too, and it cost me a weekend instead of a launch.

## How it shows up in my work

This is the same instinct behind the work I do for teams. The dashboard a content team now opens every day started as a plain question: how do I stop pulling these numbers by hand. I have shipped small automations with `Python`, `FastAPI`, and a bit of `AWS` the same way. Scratch a real itch first, then notice other people have it too.

Build for yourself, use it honestly, and let the useful ones grow up.
